  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE...Prolonged period of dry and quiet weather with \nmoderating temperatures next week leading to increasingly mild \nafternoons with cool nights.\n\nAs we head into the third week of November, you might not know it by \nlooking at the forecast. While average highs for this time of year \nare in the upper 40s and low 50s, we'll only get one day in the \nnormal range (Monday) before we begin a steady but persistent warm \nup well above average for Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday. This \ncomes courtesy of rising mid level heights and a steadily warming \nairmass beneath an approaching mid level ridge and surface high \npressure which covers almost the whole eastern half of the U.S. \nthrough at least mid-week. Even beyond that, it is likely that it \nwon't be fully displaced with a breakdown of the prevailing pattern \nand more unsettled weather until sometime next weekend at the \nearliest. Dry NW flow around the high will lead to chilly low \ntemperatures exacerbated at times by radiational cooling while highs \nwarm well above average, into the mid or even upper 60s by mid to \nlate week. Eventually a dry cold front cools the airmass down just a \nbit by Saturday but no arctic intrusions are on the horizon.",
